symmetry it sounds so simple but what is  the definition of symmetry well the word  symmetry comes from the Greek word  Sumitra meany agreement in dimensions  due proportion arrangement however in  mathematics symmetry has a more precise  definition that an object is invariant  to transformation now there are many  types of symmetry such as reflection of  symmetry a line of symmetry going  through an object which divides it into  two pieces which are mirror images of  each other and translational symmetry an  object that has translational symmetry  can be translated without changing its  overall shape and helical symmetry can  be simultaneously translated and rotated  in 3-dimensional space along a line  known as a screw axis  and scale symmetry does not change shape  when it is expanded or contracted eg  fractals  and finally rotational symmetry when an  object has rotational symmetry it can be  rotated about a fixed point without  changing the overall shape today I just  want to focus on rotational symmetry and  what are the order of rotation and angle  of rotation rotational symmetry has to  do with objects that can be rotated  about a pivot and can still coincide  with the original shape at least once in  less than a 300 degrees turn there are  also many rotational symmetrical objects  in daily life even though you may not  notice the Triskelion is also one  example of a 3-foot rotational  symmetrical object and has an order of  rotation meaning the number of matches  you can make in one rotation of three  and it also has the angle of rotation  the degree of the angle away from the  previous match to the next one of 360  over the order of rotation which is 360  over 3 equals 120 degrees there are even  four fold and fivefold shapes of this  form each named after their order of  rotation  to draw one he simply have to draw a  regular shape such as the Pentagon and  then he draw arcs inside the shape and  spirals extending from the vertices  finally you erase the first step
 

